 "reading": "Under the bounded pool both proportional gates fail. The narrow one (4 thresholds) runs away before 750 updates: 247,000 pulses a byte against a budget of 113,000, the price 17 and climbing to 126. The wide one (16) diverges after 1,500 updates, its price rising from 0.17 to 0.54, and scores 2.31 at 2,250. In the narrow run the pool reached 36 to 47 quanta, where a single gate step let in all of the value: the gates turned into on/off switches (wide open at 65% to 92% of cell-bytes) and 58% to 83% of the bodies sat at their bound, where they learn nothing. Under the lifting pool, which divides the gate's charge before any bound so that a loud stream closes gates, the narrow ramp still runs away (2.31, 2.44, 2.96; price 11.4; its open gates let in 87% to 94%). The wide ramp is stable: 0.088 ahead of the lifted plain base at 750 updates, 0.013 at 1,500 and 0.003 at 2,250, at the same pulses and nearly the same price (0.064 against 0.049). Its gates are graded: open at 37% to 39% of cell-bytes, they let in 41% to 53% of the value on average and are wide open at only 3% to 8%. The shunting gate with inner steps, whose open gates let in 76% to 83%, leads at 750 updates and trails by 0.037 at 1,500 and 0.063 at 2,250 under the lifting pool, as under the bounded one (0.045 and 0.066). Inner steps are worth 0.013 to 0.019 against the shunt whose steps fire, at every evaluation, so the energy tax is the smaller part of the shunt's loss. The gated cells that lost acted as switches; the gate that stays graded keeps pace.",
 "verdict": "Only the wide proportional gate under the lifting pool is stable and level with its plain base at 2,250 updates. It goes to a screen by slope, two seeds on the server and one on the laptop (proportional_gates_with_data.json). The narrow ramp is dropped, the bounded ramp is not used, and the shunting gate is not screened again.",
 "amendment": {
  "recorded_utc": "2026-11-23T11:55:50Z",
  "change": "The diagnosis is made again by a tool of the repository, experiments/gate_state.py with its tests, on the same weights (every stopped run's model.pt.partial: the raw weights at 2,250 updates) and on streams, as every score of the stack is taken. The figures recorded above stay as they were measured; the block below is the measure that can be reproduced.",
  "correction": "The scratch script counted the pulses of the firing populations only. It left out what the reads count for the budget themselves, the racing cells' spikes and the read pulses that they pass on: 11,457 to 13,935 pulses a byte in the five stacks that held their budget, 21,901 in the narrow ramp under the lifting pool and 70,889 in the narrow ramp under the bounded pool. Its totals (pulses_a_byte_in_windows) are low by about that much. The reading took its pulses a byte from the trainer's count, not from these totals, and every per-level figure that it quotes is within one point of the measure on streams.",
  "finding": "A gate whose steps stay inside its cell sends no pulse for them, and the budget counts none. The steps are still taken: 47,915 a byte in the wide proportional gate under the lifting pool, beside the stack's 112,663 pulses a byte, and 35,919 and 49,070 in the shunting gates with inner steps. Every report of an inner gate states these steps beside its pulses.",
  "graded or switch": "The tool also sorts every open gate by the share of the value that it lets in: up to a quarter, up to a half, up to three quarters, or more. The wide proportional gate under the lifting pool spreads over all four: 40% / 27% / 16% / 17% at the first level and 28% / 23% / 17% / 32% at the last. Every gate that lost has most of its open states in the last quarter: the shunting gates with inner steps 66% to 79% (bounded) and 66% to 79% (lifted), the wide ramp under the bounded pool 48% to 73%, the narrow ramp 79% to 90% under the lifting pool and 100% under the bounded one. This is the measure behind the reading's last sentence.",
  "measure": "experiments/gate_state.py --enwik8 data/enwik8 --split validation --bytes 65536 --streams 16 --lead 1024 --piece 512: the first 65,536 validation bytes as 16 streams, the 1,024 lead bytes of every stream left out",
